  data:  # a93d
    kind: work_item  # a93d
    title: "dead-code-maybe systematic false positives: abstract-method overrides, framework lifecycle methods, polymorphic dispatch all flagged dead (92.6% candidate rate on self-analysis)"  # a93d
    status: todo_hard  # a93d
    priority: 2  # a93d
    parent:  # a93d
    tags:  # a93d
    - dead_code  # a93d
    - polymorphism  # a93d
    - false_positive  # a93d
    - self_analysis  # a93d
    - dogfooding  # a93d
    isbefore: []  # a93d
    duplicate_of: []  # a93d
    not_duplicate_of: []  # a93d
    pr_ref:  # a93d
    description: "**Found via self-analysis dogfooding on 2026-05-16 (round 11).**\n\n**Symptom.** `hypergumbo dead-code-maybe` reports many functions/methods as \"potentially dead\" that are demonstrably live. The pattern is systematic: any method called through polymorphic dispatch, abstract-base-class interface, or framework lifecycle convention is flagged as dead because the static call graph can't see the dispatch site.\n\n**Confirmed false positives on self-analysis** (from `hypergumbo dead-code-maybe --input <baseline>`):\n\n| \"Dead\" symbol | Why it's actually live |\n|---|---|\n| `Cache.delete` (cache.py) | Public API method on tracker cache; called externally and through CLI dispatch |\n| `PythonDefUseExtractor.extract` (py_def_use.py) | Polymorphic implementation; called via base `DefUseExtractor.extract` |\n| `RustDefUseExtractor.extract` (rust_def_use.py) | Same pattern |\n| `TypeScriptDefUseExtractor.extract` (ts_def_use.py) | Same pattern |\n| `DefUseExtractor.extract` (cfg.py) | The base class itself |\n| `_AstroFileExtractor.extract` (astro.py) | Same polymorphic pattern (different extractor family) |\n| `NameMatcher.match` (name_matcher.py) | Used through dispatch |\n| `FileIndex.build`, `CfgBuilder.build`, `_TypeHierarchyIndex.build`, `_RailsIndex.build` | `.build()` lifecycle methods called by orchestrator |\n| `ItemDetail.render`, `ItemList.render`, `TrackerApp.render` (htrac-frontend) | Lit component lifecycle methods; called by the framework, not by user code |\n| `NavigationHistory.position`, `NavigationHistory.back` | Properties / public methods on tracker UI state |\n\nThese are not \"potentially dead\" — they are reachable via patterns the static call graph doesn't model.\n\n**Underlying cause.** Three concrete mechanisms produce these FPs:\n\n1. **Abstract base class dispatch.** `DefUseExtractor` defines `extract(self, ...)`; each subclass overrides it; callers go through a `base.extract(...)` interface. The static call graph emits a `calls` edge from caller→`DefUseExtractor.extract` (base), not caller→`PythonDefUseExtractor.extract` (impl). The subclass methods have zero in-edges → flagged dead.\n\n2. **Framework lifecycle methods.** Lit components define `render()`. The framework calls these via runtime dispatch. There's no static call site to find.\n\n3. **Reflective lookup / dynamic dispatch.** Methods called via `getattr(obj, \"method_name\")` or string-keyed dispatch tables have no statically resolvable call sites.\n\n**Why this matters.**\n\n1. **The output is noisy enough to be unactionable.** `dead-code-maybe` reports 3719 of 4016 production functions (92.6%) as potentially dead on self-analysis. Even with the \"maybe\" caveat in the name, a 92.6% candidate set is not a useful starting point for cleanup.\n\n2. **The patterns flagged as \"dead\" are exactly the most architecturally important code** — base-class interface methods, framework integration points, lifecycle hooks. Burying these in noise teaches the agent reader to ignore the tool output.\n\n3. **`hypergumbo`'s self-analysis flags hypergumbo's own internal interface implementations as dead.** That's a credibility problem if hypergumbo is shipped as a tool for other agents.\n\n**Severity.** Medium-high for the dead-code subcommand's utility; low-medium for the broader correctness story (it's a \"maybe\" tool by design).\n\n**Fix proposal — multiple angles, increasing scope.**\n\n1. **Surface the polymorphic-dispatch heuristic.** When a method is flagged dead, check if it's an override of a base-class method that IS reachable. If yes, mark with `dispatch_inherited=True` and demote from \"dead\" to \"live-via-dispatch\" in the report. Low effort, high signal.\n\n2. **Recognize framework lifecycle conventions.** Add a known-pattern list:\n   - Lit / web components: `render()`, `connectedCallback()`, `disconnectedCallback()`, `attributeChangedCallback()`\n   - Pytest: `test_*`, `setup_*`, `teardown_*` methods\n   - Click/Typer: methods decorated with `@command`\n   - Starlette/FastAPI: methods used as `Route(view_func=X)`\n   \n   Suppress these as \"framework dispatch — not statically callable but probably live.\"\n\n3. **Cross-reference `usage_contexts`.** Hypergumbo already detects route handlers etc. as `usage_contexts`. If a method appears as the `view_func` of a `usage_context`, exclude it from the dead list.\n\n4. **Annotate with confidence/uncertainty.** Add a `dispatch_hint` field per dead-code entry: \"abstract method\", \"framework lifecycle\", \"property\", \"no callers found (genuinely possibly dead)\". Lets agent consumers filter to the actionable subset.\n\nRecommended: (1) + (3) immediately (cheap, high signal); (2) + (4) as a follow-up.\n\n**Test gap.** Snapshot test on the self-analysis fixture: assert that none of the known-live methods (concrete list above) appear in the dead-code report. This pins the regression boundary.\n\n**Cross-references.**\n\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/cli.py::cmd_dead_code_maybe` — the consumer\n- `packages/hypergumbo-core/src/hypergumbo_core/dead_code.py` (if it exists) — likely the implementation\n- `INV-mofav-...` (filed round 10) — Python analyzer's nested-function blindness; complementary call-graph completeness gap; fixing INV-mofav would also reduce dead-code FPs (because some methods reach others through nested helpers that go uncaptured today)\n- `WI-fanun-...` (filed round 9) — fingerprint field mostly null; sibling \"tool features exist but don't work on real code\" pattern"  # a93d
    fields: {}  # a93d
